MappedParameterThree = {
    ("EngagementCodeList", NAMEOF ( 'Project_snapshot_Fact'[EngagementCodeList] ), 0),
    ("AlphaCode", NAMEOF ( 'Project_snapshot_Fact'[AlphaCode] ), 1),
    ("CustodianDisplayName", NAMEOF ( 'Project_snapshot_Fact'[CustodianDisplayName] ), 2),
    ("EFileName", NAMEOF ( 'Project_snapshot_Fact'[EFileName] ), 3)
}
// CONFIRMED bindings for this visual's 3 hierarchy levels (per the report
// author, not yet independently re-verified live against this repo's own
// tooling -- see the README's "Known limitations"):
//   Level1 -> 'MappedParameter'[Category]        (aster plot's own field
//                                                  parameter, reused)
//   Level2 -> 'MappedParameterTwo'[Category2]     (waffle chart's own
//                                                  field parameter, reused)
//   Level3 -> 'MappedParameterThree'[Category3]   (new -- this table)
//
// A THIRD, independent field parameter over the exact same 4 candidate
// columns as visuals/aster-plot/dax/category-field-parameter.dax
// (MappedParameter) and visuals/waffle-chart/dax/category-two-field-parameter.dax
// (MappedParameterTwo) -- lets a report viewer swap which column drives
// this visual's Level3 role via its own slicer, independently of whatever
// MappedParameter/MappedParameterTwo are currently sliced to.
//
// Level1 and Level2 are NOT new field parameters -- they reuse the aster
// plot's and waffle chart's existing ones directly. This means slicing
// MappedParameter in the report moves the aster plot's Category AND this
// visual's Level1 together (shared state, not a coincidence -- same table
// referenced by both specs), and likewise MappedParameterTwo moves the
// waffle chart's Category and this visual's Level2 together. Worth being
// deliberate about whether that shared-slicer coupling is wanted before
// wiring this up in the report -- if Level1/Level2 need to move
// independently of the aster plot/waffle chart, they'd need their own
// separate field parameters instead of reusing these two.
//
// Since all 3 field parameters share the exact same 4 candidate names,
// and a field parameter's resolved key can't be renamed per-visual (same
// finding as MappedParameter/MappedParameterTwo -- see
// category-field-parameter.dax and category-two-field-parameter.dax), a
// naive isValid() cascade checking the same 4 candidates in the same
// order for all 3 roles cannot tell them apart. The spec's field-mapping
// adapter (visuals/circle-packing/spec/circle-packing.vg.json) fixes this
// with a 3-way exclusion cascade -- Level1 resolves first (highest-priority
// candidate present, same as always), Level2 resolves to the
// highest-priority *remaining* candidate Level1 didn't already claim, and
// Level3 to the highest-priority candidate neither Level1 nor Level2
// claimed. See the README's "Field mapping" section for the full
// mechanism, including the 'All' fallback when a level's own field
// parameter isn't distinctly sliced.
//
// If you ever reference this table's own columns in a measure, use MAX,
// not SELECTEDVALUE or ALLSELECTED -- same composite-key constraint as
// MappedParameter/MappedParameterTwo, see
// visuals/aster-plot/dax/measure-a-color-gradient.dax for the full
// writeup. This table's own label column is stated as "Category3" (per
// the report author) -- reference it as 'MappedParameterThree'[Category3].
// Not yet independently confirmed the way "Category2" was for
// MappedParameterTwo (that one was verified live via the Fields pane) --
// worth a quick check before relying on the exact column name.
